{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Los Angeles Angels opened the season with a burst of energy that suggested a team capable of competing in a brutal AL West. Danny Espinosa's three-run homer on April 4th set the tone, and Albert Pujols delivered clutch singles in back-to-back games to help the Angels build an early 14-13 April record — treading water, but alive. Mike Trout was his usual transcendent self, and unexpected contributors like Cliff Pennington and Jefry Marte stepped up at key moments to keep the ship afloat.\n\nMay and June brought inconsistency and a bullpen that could not be trusted in close games. Pitchers like Bud Norris and Deolis Guerra were victimized for crucial home runs, and the Angels spent those months hovering right around .500, unable to build any sustained momentum. A 14-15 May and a 14-14 June told the story of a team always taking one step forward and one step back, talented enough to hang around but not disciplined enough to separate themselves from mediocrity.\n\nJuly proved the cruelest month, as the Angels went just 9-13 and watched their playoff hopes dim considerably. The nadir came on July 30th when Bud Norris surrendered a Steve Pearce grand slam — the single most damaging play of the entire season by win probability — a gut punch that encapsulated everything going wrong with the pitching staff. Yet the team responded with its finest stretch of baseball in August, going 18-10 and surging back into relevance. Andrelton Simmons launched a pivotal three-run homer, Cliff Pennington hit a grand slam, and Mike Trout's clutch double against Seattle underscored what made this roster dangerous when everything clicked. Manny Machado's two grand slams against Angels pitching were maddening setbacks, but the Angels' bats largely overcame them during this remarkable August run.\n\nSeptember, however, delivered the fatal verdict. Going 10-17 down the stretch, the Angels could not sustain their August magic. The bullpen continued to bleed late-game leads, and a season that had briefly flirted with October baseball collapsed into a familiar 80-82 finish. The Angels scored almost exactly as many runs as they allowed — 710 to 709 — a statistical portrait of a team perfectly calibrated for irrelevance, talented enough to compete on any given night but not good enough to win the games that mattered most when the calendar ran out.",
